   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The functions \\( f \\) and \\( g \\) are differentiable, with \\( f(-1) = 1 \\), \\( f'(-1) = 6 \\), \\( g(-1) = -3 \\), \\( g'(-1) = 2 \\). Let \\( h(x) = \\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} \\). Find the value of \\( h'(-1) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         -20,
         9
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Record the values at \\( x = -1 \\): \\( f(-1) = 1 \\), \\( f'(-1) = 6 \\), \\( g(-1) = -3 \\), \\( g'(-1) = 2 \\).",
         "mathjson": 6
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "By the quotient rule, \\( \\left(\\frac{f}{g}\\right)'(-1) = \\frac{f'(-1)g(-1) - f(-1)g'(-1)}{[g(-1)]^{2}} \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Substitute: \\( \\frac{(6)(-3) - (1)(2)}{(-3)^{2}} = \\frac{-20}{9} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            -20,
            9
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( h'(-1) = - \\frac{20}{9} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            -20,
            9
         ]
      }
   ],
